| **BRIEF-1 IST null result** (ORR 0/10 at interim) | 35% | HIGH — primary valuation driver; Series B step-up depends on clinical signal | Pre-specified interim stopping rule limits downside. Licensing deal (Stream 1) is independent of IST result — pharma buys the hypothesis, not the outcome. Negative IST data is still publishable and informs next iteration. |
| **Merck KGaA does not engage** (BRIEF-1 ROFN) | 40% | MEDIUM — delays first deal; does not kill it | Competitor list identified in parallel. 60-day ROFN is structural urgency, not a dependency. If Merck KGaA passes, approach competitor on day 61. |
| **ZEB1/CD274 TMA negative** (BRIEF-3 gap not closed) | 30% | MEDIUM — BRIEF-3 delayed; does not kill it | If TMA is negative, commission spatial transcriptomics (10x Visium) in BrM specimens — higher resolution, higher cost ($80–150K), but may detect co-expression at the cellular level that bulk IHC misses. |
| **PTEN-KO validation negative** (BRIEF-2 stays computational hypothesis) | 60% | LOW-MEDIUM — BRIEF-2 rNPV stays at $3.5M base; convergent resistance claim retains value | Negative result is disclosed honestly. BRIEF-2 is narrowed, not closed. Convergent resistance patient selection logic (PTEN-null patients who have failed 3 drug classes) is still licensable at lower value. |
| **No CNS-penetrant NRF2 inhibitor identified** (BRIEF-4 drug gap) | 50% | LOW — BRIEF-4 stays as option value; does not affect Series A/B | File broad method claim. Monitor pipeline quarterly. BRIEF-4 is the Series C asset, not the Series A/B driver. |
| **Alice/Mayo patent eligibility challenge** (B1/B4 computational claims) | 25% | MEDIUM — computational method claims may be challenged in US | Include system claims (CrisPRO platform hardware + software) alongside method claims. Emphasize specific technical implementation (7D vectors, PATH A formula) not abstract idea. Conduct Alice/Mayo analysis before filing. |
| **Repare/Roche deal collapse precedent** | OCCURRED (2024) | MEDIUM — Roche terminated camonsertib deal; Repare cut 25% workforce | CrisPRO's asset-light model is structurally different from Repare. CrisPRO never holds an IND. If pharma terminates a licensing deal, CrisPRO retains the IP and approaches the next buyer. The Repare collapse is a cautionary tale for drug developers, not IP licensors. |
| **Biossil competitive entry** ($70M raised, April 2026) | OCCURRING | LOW — different failure mode (wrong indication, not wrong patient selection); different model (they own the drug) | CrisPRO's moat is the patient selection logic + companion diagnostic claim. Biossil acquires the molecule and runs the trial. They are not competing for the same assets. |
| **Single deal-ready asset concentration** | CURRENT STATE | HIGH — if BRIEF-1 deal fails, Series A is delayed | Three revenue streams reduce concentration risk. Stream 2 (IST SRA) and Stream 3 (validation packages) generate revenue independent of Stream 1 licensing deals. BRIEF-3 gap closure (Month 3–4) creates second deal-ready asset quickly. |

### The Honest Bottom Line

CrisPRO has one deal-ready asset (BRIEF-1), one experiment away from a second (BRIEF-3), and two assets that require validation. The $500M Series B requires: BRIEF-1 deal signed + BRIEF-3 deal signed + IST interim data showing any signal + BRIEF-4 drug identified or BRIEF-2 validated. That is four milestones across 30–36 months.

The risk is real. The concentration in BRIEF-1 is real. The PTEN-KO null result is real. The NRF2 drug gap is real.

What is also real: $1.95 billion in decoded pharma spend as the scientific foundation. Two CRISPR-validated SL assets. A live pharma partner. A production loop that costs $50–200K per trial and generates $3.35M in expected value per trial decoded. A business model that never requires an IND, never runs a trial, and scales with every failure in the literature.

The graveyard has 500 more trials. The engine is running. The $500M is not a fantasy — it is a milestone sequence with defined experiments, defined deal triggers, and defined valuation inflection points.

**The only way this plan fails is if every experiment is negative, every BD approach is rejected, and every IST shows zero signal. The probability of that outcome is not zero. It is also not the base case.**

